/// \defgroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks W3D gadget drawing: tab control, list box, push button, vertical slider and window borders
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief The W3D device layer's draw callbacks for tab controls, list
///        boxes, push buttons and image-look vertical sliders, their text
///        and overlay helpers, and the loader for the window border art.
///
/// This group continues \ref grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw with
/// the other gadget families. The model is the same. A gadget is a
/// GameWindow with a gadget style and a block of gadget-specific data (see
/// \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets); its input and system behaviour lives in the
/// game engine, but its drawing lives in the W3D device layer, in one
/// source file per gadget family, as one function per look. The functions
/// documented here are:
/// - tab control: W3DGadgetTabControlDraw() (colour look) and
///   W3DGadgetTabControlImageDraw() (image look);
/// - list box: W3DGadgetListBoxDraw() (colour background) and
///   W3DGadgetListBoxImageDraw() (image background), which share
///   drawListBoxText() for the rows and drawHiliteBar() for the selected-row
///   bar;
/// - push button: W3DGadgetPushButtonDraw() (colour look),
///   W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne() (one image per state) and
///   W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawThree() (left end, repeating middle, right
///   end), with getButtonTextColors(), drawButtonText() and
///   drawRadialOverlay() as helpers. W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw() chooses
///   among a vertical routine (described with the dispatcher) and the two
///   image routines here, and is described with the other push button code
///   in \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists;
/// - vertical slider: W3DGadgetVerticalSliderImageDraw(), the image look
///   only. The colour look of a vertical slider is served by the horizontal
///   slider's colour draw function (see the check box group);
/// - window borders: initBorders(), which looks up the twelve border
///   pieces that bordered windows are drawn with.
///
/// \par How a draw callback is reached
/// Target fact, read from the retail slot bodies: slot 3 of each gadget's
/// window vtable hands the window and its instance data to the matching
/// W3DGadget*Draw function. The tab control, list box (both looks), colour
/// push button, push button dispatcher and vertical slider callbacks on this
/// page are each reached from such a slot 3 body; the two image push button
/// routines other than the dispatcher are called by the dispatcher. Inferred,
/// not proven by retail data: that these gadget windows are GameWindow
/// subclasses whose slot 3 is a virtual draw method (the same arrangement
/// the check box group describes), and the class and method names given to
/// those slot bodies, which follow the bodies rather than any retail symbol.
/// This page does not describe those classes further.
///
/// \par Where the names come from
/// The function lexicon is the table that maps callback names to native
/// functions, so that data can name a callback (see
/// \ref sub_common_services_namekeys). The names of the tab control, list
/// box, colour push button, push button dispatcher and vertical slider
/// callbacks are target facts: retail's function lexicon lists each one by
/// name. The other names here (drawListBoxText, drawHiliteBar,
/// getButtonTextColors, drawButtonText, initBorders and the two image push
/// button routines the dispatcher calls) belong to functions with no retail
/// symbol: they are donor names, from the Zero Hour source or from BFME 1, and
/// are not proven by retail data. The name drawRadialOverlay is different
/// again: it is a reconstruction label for a file-local helper. It has no
/// retail symbol, it does not occur in the Zero Hour source (which has no
/// radial overlay), and BFME 1 holds only an address-suffixed helper for the
/// same job, so it is not a donor name. Zero Hour: the same callbacks are
/// plain functions listed by name in the W3D function lexicon's draw table,
/// and each window holds its draw callback as a function pointer.
///
/// \par Common drawing model
/// - Draw data. Each window has three arrays of nine slots (enabled,
///   disabled and highlighted), and each slot holds an image, a colour and
///   a border colour. What a slot means is fixed by the gadget. The slot
///   names below follow Zero Hour's gadget headers. Where a BFME 2 body on
///   this page reads a slot, it uses it in the role given; of the slots
///   given a role below, the only one no body here reads is the tab
///   control's slot 0.
///   - Tab control: slots 1 to 8 are the eight tabs. Slot 0 is the window
///     background in Zero Hour; the BFME 2 default-draw helper that would
///     paint it is not confirmed here.
///   - List box: slot 0 is the list background, and slots 1 to 4 are the
///     selected row's left end, right end, centre and small centre images,
///     slot 1's colours being the colour variant of the selection.
///   - Colour push button: slot 0 is the normal look and slot 1 the selected
///     look. Single-image push button: the same two slots. Three-piece push
///     button: slots 0, 5 and 6 are the normal left end, middle and right
///     end, and slots 1, 3 and 4 the selected ones.
///   - Vertical slider: top end, bottom end, repeating centre and small
///     repeating centre in slots 0 to 3.
/// - Which array. For the colour push button, the three-piece push button,
///   the list boxes and the vertical slider, the window's status (the
///   enabled flag) and its instance state (the highlighted bit) choose it:
///   not enabled gives the disabled array, then a highlighted window gives
///   the highlighted array, otherwise the enabled array. (For the push
///   buttons the selected bit then chooses between slots.) The single-image
///   push button follows the rule only in part. In its overlay-state mode
///   it takes slot 0 of the enabled array whatever the state and shows the
///   state by tint and the cameo overlays instead. With the no-state-images
///   status it also takes slot 0 and skips the state tint, and only the
///   overlay-state status gates the overlays, so a button with
///   no-state-images alone shows no state through this routine's image,
///   tint or overlays. In its plain mode an enabled, not highlighted,
///   selected button reads slot 1 of the highlighted array (see
///   W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne()). Tab controls are a different
///   exception: they ignore the window's own state and choose per tab from
///   the tab data (see W3DGadgetTabControlDraw()).
/// - Primitives. Everything is drawn in screen pixels through the window
///   manager (image, outlined rectangle and filled rectangle calls, font
///   height, colour packing), the display (clip region, clipping on and
///   off, video buffer, clock and image-mode draws) and the window's
///   display string (font, word wrap, text colour, size, draw with a shadow
///   offset). None of these routines scales anything for resolution.
/// - Order. The window's own background and border come first where the
///   routine draws them at all, then gadget content, then labels, then the
///   push button extras (video buffer, overlay image, clock, outline and
///   count label; the order differs a little between the push button
///   routines).
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Everything here is client-side GUI drawing. None of it writes
///   game-logic state. The only read of game state found on this page is in
///   W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne(), where the inverse clock colour
///   depends on a flag in the local player's data and on a global colour
///   setting. Everything else the routines write is GUI-side: button data
///   (the clock request), display strings (font, colour, clip region, count
///   text) and the border image table. Since that one read changes nothing,
///   changing these routines cannot desynchronise a multiplayer game or
///   affect the logic random numbers, CRC checks, saves or replays.
/// - Data-driven, from .wnd layouts: the window's size, position, font and
///   text; its text colours (TEXTCOLOR); the image offset (IMAGEOFFSET);
///   every colour and image
///   (the ENABLEDDRAWDATA, DISABLEDDRAWDATA and HILITEDRAWDATA blocks, see
///   parseDrawData(), which resolves image names through the mapped image
///   collection and therefore needs them defined in MappedImage INI
///   blocks); the tab control's layout (the TABCONTROLDATA block) and the
///   list box's columns (the LISTBOXDATA block). Which look a window gets
///   is chosen when the gadget is created. The check box group describes
///   that choice, made on the IMAGE status flag; it was not traced for the
///   gadgets documented here.
/// - Status flags. A layout writes a window's status as a token list, and the
///   reconstruction's table of tokens (WindowStatusNames) is Zero Hour's
///   list. Retail's own table was not read for this page, so which tokens
///   BFME 2 accepts is unconfirmed. With that caveat, BORDER, SEE_THRU,
///   ONE_LINE, WRAP_CENTERED, USE_OVERLAY_STATES and ALWAYS_COLOR are tokens
///   in that list whose flags the routines here test. A layout value is not
///   the whole story for the control bar's command buttons:
///   ControlBar::init sets the status bit with the overlay-state value on the
///   command button template and on the right-hand upgrade cameo windows, and
///   ControlBar::updateContextCommand, on each pass over the command
///   windows it processes, clears four status bits and then sets some of
///   them from the command's availability. It skips a window that has no
///   command and a window that is already hidden while its command's
///   availability is hidden, and leaves the skipped window's bits as they
///   were. The four are the not-ready and always-colour
///   bits and, matched by number only, the two highest bits of the status
///   word, which the push button reconstruction labels no-state-images and
///   gray-overlay. In that body the gray-overlay value is set for three of
///   the availability results that disable the window (their enumerator
///   names were not established here), and the no-state-images value is set
///   on a window that the function disables because the commands belong to
///   a unit the local player does not control. One command type takes an
///   early branch that sets the gray-overlay or the always-colour value
///   instead. On the windows the function processes, these four bits are
///   therefore run-time state, and a layout value for them does not
///   survive a pass. No layout token for the no-state-images or
///   gray-overlay bits was confirmed. The push button routines also test a
///   flashing status. Who sets it in BFME 2 was not traced; in Zero Hour the
///   control bar's code sets and clears it at run time.
/// - Set from code at run time, not from layouts: list rows and cells (see
///   GadgetListBoxAddEntryText() and GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage()), the
///   push button extras (clock, overlay image, outline, count) and the
///   active tab.
/// - Hard-coded: all geometry (insets, offsets, gaps), the eight-tab limit,
///   the fixed art names looked up by the push button and border code, the
///   48-pixel base size that radial overlays are scaled from, and the
///   overlay tints.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - This is part of the seam between the window system and the renderer.
///   A new renderer can keep these functions if it implements the window
///   manager's rectangle and image primitives, the display's clip, video
///   and clock calls and the display string interface, or it can replace
///   them one look at a time.
/// - Clipping is a single region: the routines that clip set the display's
///   region and later turn clipping off, they never restore a previous
///   region.
/// - Several routines cache looked-up images for the life of the process
///   (the push button's overlay icons, the border pieces) and nothing
///   refreshes them. A port that reloads the mapped image collection has to
///   invalidate those pointers.
/// - None of the routines on this page scales for resolution. The horizontal
///   slider image draw (see the check box group) is the one gadget draw found
///   that does, among the gadget families documented in these two groups;
///   for the rest, resolution independence has to come from how windows and
///   fonts are sized when a layout is loaded (see
///   \ref sub_gui_apt_remastering).
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw,
///      \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets, \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists


/// \file Gadget/W3DGadgetTabControlDraw.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks
/// \brief W3D device layer: the colour-look tab control draw callback.
///
/// The file also defines a run of one-line readers of single draw-data
/// fields. They are not described here.


/// \fn void W3DGadgetTabControlDraw(GameWindow *tabControl, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for colour-look tab controls: the default-draw step
///        and the window border, then one outlined, filled rectangle per
///        tab.
///
/// The retail function lexicon (the callback-name table, see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks) lists this callback by name,
/// and slot 3 of the colour-look tab control's window vtable calls it. It draws
/// the tab strip only. The panes that the tabs switch between are child
/// windows and are drawn on their own, and no tab label text is drawn here.
/// GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion() fixes where the strip sits, and
/// GadgetTabControlShowSubPane() decides which tab is active.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Calls the window's default-draw step first. BFME 2 reaches it through
///   a GameWindow member; the reconstructed body of that member runs the
///   window's own draw callback when one is set, and otherwise draws through
///   a helper object the window holds. Zero Hour called its free function
///   W3DGameWinDefaultDraw here, with the comment "draw the background",
///   and that function paints slot 0 of the draw data (the image when the
///   IMAGE status is set, else the colour and border). That the BFME 2
///   helper paints the same slot is not confirmed here.
/// - Draws the window border (GameWindow::winDrawBorder) when the window has
///   the border status and not the see-through status.
/// - Walks the tabs from the strip's top-left corner, which is the window
///   origin plus the strip limits kept in the tab data. The step is one tab
///   width along x when the tabs sit on the top or bottom edge, and one tab
///   height along y otherwise.
/// - A tab exists when the tab count reaches it, up to eight. Its state
///   comes from the tab data, not from the window: a pane with its disabled
///   flag set uses the disabled array, else the active tab uses the
///   highlighted array, else the enabled array. The slot is the tab's index
///   plus one.
/// - If the slot's border colour is defined the tab's rectangle is outlined
///   in it, one pixel wide, and then, if its colour is defined, the inside
///   is filled with it, one pixel in on every side. Either may be left
///   undefined.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The tab count, tab width and height, edge, orientation and per-pane
///   disabled flags come from the TABCONTROLDATA block. Of these the draw
///   callbacks read the count, size, edge and pane-disabled flags, not the
///   orientation. The active tab and the strip limits that the walk also
///   reads are not fields of that block. Tab colours come from slots 1 to 8
///   of the three draw-data arrays, which the BFME 2 body reads. Zero Hour:
///   slot 0 is the window background; the BFME 2 default-draw helper that
///   would paint it is not confirmed here, so do not assume slot 0 styles
///   the background without testing. The highlighted array styles the active
///   tab, not a tab under the mouse.
/// - A disabled window still draws its tabs from the enabled array unless
///   the individual pane is flagged disabled, because the window's own
///   enabled flag is not consulted for tabs.
/// - At most eight tabs are drawn, whatever the tab count says.
/// - Neither tab control draw callback draws any text.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The routine uses only the window manager's outline and fill rectangle
/// primitives, in window pixels with a fixed one-pixel line, and never
/// scales.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control window. Its user data must be the tab
///                   control data; nothing checks it, and a null pointer is
///                   dereferenced.
/// \param instData   The window's instance data, passed on to the
///                   default-draw step.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's W3DTabControl.cpp draws the same geometry and picks
///       colours the same way. The only behavioural difference found is the
///       default-draw step, which Zero Hour calls directly.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a target fact (retail's function lexicon lists it; see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks). The behaviour is read
/// from the BFME 2 body and agrees with Zero Hour's W3DTabControl.cpp and
/// GadgetTabControl.h, which supply the slot meanings.
/// \see W3DGadgetTabControlImageDraw, GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion,
///      GadgetTabControlShowSubPane


/// \file Gadget/W3DGadgetTabControlImageDraw.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks
/// \brief W3D device layer: the image-look tab control draw callback.
///
/// The file also defines a run of one-line readers of single draw-data
/// image fields. They are not described here.


/// \fn void W3DGadgetTabControlImageDraw(GameWindow *tabControl, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for image-look tab controls: the default-draw step
///        and the window border, then one image per tab.
///
/// The retail function lexicon (the callback-name table, see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks) lists this callback by name,
/// and slot 3 of the image-look tab control's window vtable calls it. It has the
/// same structure as W3DGadgetTabControlDraw() and differs only in what a
/// tab is drawn with.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Default-draw step, border and tab walk are exactly those of
///   W3DGadgetTabControlDraw(): the strip starts at the window origin plus
///   the strip limits, and each tab is one tab width (top and bottom edges)
///   or one tab height (side edges) further on.
/// - For each tab up to the tab count, and at most eight, the image comes
///   from slot index plus one of the disabled array (pane flagged
///   disabled), the highlighted array (the active tab) or the enabled
///   array.
/// - A tab whose image is missing is skipped, with no fallback to another
///   state's image. Otherwise the image is stretched over the whole tab
///   rectangle and drawn with an explicit colour argument of -1 (all bits
///   set: white and fully opaque in the packed ARGB format the window
///   manager uses). Zero Hour leaves the argument out and takes the same
///   value from the declared default. No outline and no inset are added; the
///   art carries its own edges.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The images come from slots 1 to 8 of the three draw-data arrays, by name
/// from the mapped image collection. An image is stretched to the tab size
/// from the TABCONTROLDATA block, so art meant for another tab size is
/// scaled, not cropped. As in the colour look, the active tab uses the
/// highlighted array and a flagged pane the disabled array.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control window. Its user data must be the tab
///                   control data; nothing checks it.
/// \param instData   The window's instance data, passed on to the
///                   default-draw step.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a target fact (retail's function lexicon lists it; see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks). The behaviour is read
/// from the BFME 2 body. Zero Hour's W3DTabControl.cpp has the same image
/// look, and its GadgetTabControl.h gives the slot meanings.
/// \see W3DGadgetTabControlDraw, GadgetTabControlShowSubPane


/// \file Gadget/W3DListBox.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks
/// \brief W3D device layer: the list box draw callbacks, the row drawing
///        and the selected-row bar.
///
/// The file also holds small inline readers of single draw-data fields, and
/// one further non-inline reader of a single enabled draw-data field whose
/// exact meaning is not established here. They are not described here.


/// \fn static void drawHiliteBar(const Image *left, const Image *right, const Image *center, Int startX, Int startY, Int endX, Int endY)
/// \brief Draws the selected-row bar of a list box from three images: a left
///        end, a right end and a repeating centre.
///
/// File-static helper of drawListBoxText(), which calls it for every
/// selected row drawn with images.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The bar's width is endX minus startX, widened if necessary to the sum
///   of the two end images' widths, so a narrow row can draw past endX. Its
///   height is endY minus startY, and every piece is stretched to it.
/// - The centre image is tiled at its own width between the left end and the
///   right end, as many whole tiles as fit. The left end is then drawn at
///   the left edge and the right end flush with the right edge.
/// - BFME 2 leaves the remainder alone. If the gap is not a multiple of the
///   centre image's width, the last few pixels before the right end are
///   not drawn. Zero Hour filled that gap with a fourth image, the small
///   centre, clipped to the gap; the BFME 2 body takes no such image. After
///   the centre tiles it sets the display clip region to the full gap
///   between the two end images (from the right edge of the left end to the
///   left edge of the right end, at the bar's height). That width is the
///   whole gap, not just the span the tiles covered, because the body does
///   not recompute it after the tile loop. It then calls
///   enableClipping(FALSE) without drawing anything clipped to that region,
///   and draws the left and right end images. What that pair of calls does to
///   the end images on screen is not established here: no BFME 2 body for
///   those two display calls was read, and nothing in this routine shows that
///   clipping was already off at this point.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Art for the bar should make the centre image's width divide the gaps
/// that the list's width produces, or accept a thin seam before the right
/// end. The caller still requires the small centre image to be present
/// before it uses this bar, although this function does not use it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The centre width is used as a divisor, so a centre image of zero width
/// would divide by zero.
///
/// \param left    Left end image. Its width and the right end's set the minimum
///                bar width.
/// \param right   Right end image.
/// \param center  Repeating centre image. Must not be zero pixels wide.
/// \param startX  Left edge of the bar, in screen pixels.
/// \param startY  Top edge of the bar.
/// \param endX    Right edge the bar is meant to reach.
/// \param endY    Bottom edge of the bar.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is Zero Hour's. Zero Hour's version takes a fourth image and
/// draws the small centre pieces; BFME 2's takes three (see the signature
/// above). Zero Hour sets the same clip region and then draws the small
/// centre pieces clipped to it. The tiling and the clip region are read from
/// the BFME 2 body.
/// \see drawListBoxText


/// \fn static void drawListBoxText(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData, Int x, Int y, Int width, Int height, Bool useImages)
/// \brief Draws a list box's visible rows: for each row the selection
///        highlight if it is selected, then the text or image in each
///        column.
///
/// File-static helper of W3DGadgetListBoxDraw() and
/// W3DGadgetListBoxImageDraw(), which call it last. Both callers pass the
/// area below the title, moved down four pixels with the height reduced by
/// four, and both pass true for the image flag. The rows, their heights and
/// the scroll position are the list box data that the gadget code
/// (GadgetListBoxAddEntryText(), GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage() and the
/// list's system callback) maintains.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Visible range. Rows are walked from the first. Each row records the
///   running total of the heights up to its bottom; rows whose total is
///   still above the scroll position are skipped, and the walk stops at
///   the entry count or when the previous row already ends below the
///   bottom of the display area. The scroll position is in pixels.
///   Consecutive rows are one pixel further apart than their heights.
/// - Selection. In a multiple-selection list the row is selected if its
///   index is in the selection array, which ends at the first negative
///   entry. Otherwise, when the list has an alternate selection enabled
///   (two flags in the BFME 2 list data, both must be set), the row is
///   selected if it is the alternate position; else if it is the normal
///   selected position. A third flag in the list data suppresses the
///   highlight altogether. What sets these three flags and the alternate
///   position is not established here; Zero Hour has none of them.
/// - Highlight with images (the only path the callers use). The four images
///   come from slots 1 to 4 of the disabled, highlighted or enabled array.
///   The rectangle is the area's width by the row's height plus its
///   one-pixel gap, clamped vertically to the list's inner clip box. If all
///   four images exist drawHiliteBar() draws the bar, starting one pixel in;
///   otherwise, if the centre image exists (whichever of the other three
///   are missing), the centre image is stretched over the rectangle, also
///   starting one pixel in; otherwise nothing is drawn.
/// - Highlight with colours. The border and fill colours of slot 1 draw an
///   outline and, one pixel inside it, a fill; either may be undefined.
///   The callers never reach this path, so a list box needs its
///   selected-row images in the draw data to show any highlight.
/// - Cells. For each column the clip box is the column's width and the
///   row's height, clamped vertically to the inner clip box. For the clip
///   box and for text alignment, a column's width is its stored pixel
///   width, except in a single-column list whose scroll bar is hidden,
///   where it is the area width minus three. Image cells never use that
///   exception: they size and centre themselves from the stored column
///   width, and every column's left edge advances by its stored width.
///   - Text cells: the display string is set to one line when the window has
///     the one-line status, takes the window's font, the column clip box and
///     the cell's colour with a black shadow, and is measured. The x
///     position is the standard five pixel inset, or, by cell alignment,
///     right-aligned (alignment 1, keeping the same inset on the right) or
///     centred (alignment 2) in the column. The two fall back to the left
///     inset on different conditions. Right alignment falls back when the
///     text is wider than the column width minus the five pixel inset.
///     Centring falls back when its offset, the column width minus the text
///     width, divided by two in integer arithmetic, is negative: a
///     difference of minus one truncates to zero, so the text must be at
///     least two pixels wider than the column. The y position centres the
///     text in the row, never above the row's top. It is drawn with a
///     one-pixel shadow offset.
///   - Image cells (a cell that holds an image): the size is the cell's own
///     width and height where set, else the stored column width and the
///     row's height; the first column loses a pixel of width; an image
///     narrower than the stored column width or shorter than the row is
///     centred in it; the x position is never left of the inner clip box.
///     The image is drawn with the cell's colour as a tint under the display
///     clip region, and only when the clip box has some height.
/// - After every row that is processed, clipping is turned off, so the
///   display is left unclipped. Rows skipped above the scroll position never
///   reach that call, and a list with no row to process never calls it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Row content is set from code; the layout supplies the columns
///   (LISTBOXDATA), the window font and the selected-row art.
/// - Draw and hit-test are separate pieces of code, and they must stay in
///   step. Both callers start the rows four pixels below the area top, where
///   the area top is the window top moved down for a title. The list box hit
///   test (getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord, which turns a mouse position into a
///   row) measures from the window top moved down, when there is a title, by
///   the title's font height plus one pixel; apart from the scroll position it
///   applies no other vertical adjustment. By reading both bodies
///   (not observed on screen), the drawn rows therefore start four pixels
///   lower than the hit test places them without a title or in the colour
///   look, and three pixels lower in the image look with a title, because
///   that callback moves the area down by the font height alone, without the
///   extra pixel the hit test adds. Changing the geometry in one without the
///   other shifts clicks relative to rows.
/// - Cell alignment is an extra word in each BFME 2 cell and is reset
///   whenever the cell is refilled (see the gadget accessors group).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The list box text cells are display strings owned by the rows. On every
/// draw this routine sets each drawn text cell's clip region and colours;
/// it switches the font only when the string's font differs from the
/// window's font, and resets the word wrap to zero only for windows with
/// the one-line status. A replacement text renderer must tolerate those
/// changes being made to a string between draws. This routine takes each
/// row's height from the row record and does not compute it. The routine that
/// computes it (computeTotalHeight in the list box gadget code, outside this
/// page) makes a row as tall as its tallest cell: a text cell contributes
/// its measured text height, or the window font's height when the window
/// has the one-line status, and an image cell contributes its stored height
/// plus one pixel, or the window font's height when that stored height is
/// not positive. A list box image cell's stored width and height are scaled
/// by the display resolution over 1024 by 768 (width by the horizontal
/// resolution, height by the vertical) when the entry is added, in the
/// list box gadget's image-entry code, also outside this page. The cell
/// sizes this routine reads for image cells therefore already carry that
/// scaling, while it adds none of its own. Clipping uses the display's
/// single clip region.
///
/// \param window    The list box window. Its user data must be the list box
///                  data.
/// \param instData  The window's instance data (state bits, font).
/// \param x         Left edge of the row area in screen pixels.
/// \param y         Top of the row area. The caller has already added four.
/// \param width     Width of the row area; the scroll bar is already taken
///                  off.
/// \param height    Height of the row area.
/// \param useImages True to highlight selected rows with images, false with
///                  colours.
///
/// \note BFME 2 differences from Zero Hour found by comparing the two
///       bodies include: the alternate selection and hide-selection flags;
///       the three-image bar, and the stretched-centre fallback when the
///       highlight images are incomplete but the centre image exists (Zero
///       Hour draws nothing unless all four images exist); text set through
///       a separate colour call, positioned by the cell's alignment and
///       centred vertically (Zero Hour draws at the row top, left-aligned);
///       and image cells drawn without Zero Hour's one-pixel downward nudge
///       and only for a non-empty clip box. The list is not claimed to be
///       complete.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is Zero Hour's. The list and cell field uses (alternate
/// selection, hide-selection, alignment) are target facts from the retail
/// body, but their names in the reconstruction are local labels.
/// \see drawHiliteBar, W3DGadgetListBoxDraw, W3DGadgetListBoxImageDraw


/// \fn void W3DGadgetListBoxDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for list boxes with a colour background: optional
///        title, outline, fill, then the rows.
///
/// The retail function lexicon (the callback-name table, see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks) lists this callback by name,
/// and slot 3 of the colour-look list box's window vtable calls it. Only the
/// background is a colour look; the selected row is drawn with images (see
/// drawListBoxText()).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Colours (background, border, title text, title text border) come from
///   the disabled, highlighted or enabled array and the window's matching
///   text colours.
/// - If the instance data has a title, it takes the window's font, is drawn
///   at one pixel in from the window's top-left, and the list area moves
///   down by the font height plus one.
/// - The border is an outline of the list area and the background a fill one
///   pixel inside it; either is skipped when undefined.
/// - If the list has a scroll bar that is not hidden, the area width loses
///   the scroll bar's width plus three, so rows do not run under it. The
///   scroll bar's up button, down button and slider are child windows with
///   their own draw callbacks.
/// - Calls drawListBoxText() for the rows, starting four pixels below the
///   area top.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The window background comes from slot 0 of the three draw-data arrays;
/// the title is the window's text.
///
/// \param window   The list box window.
/// \param instData Its instance data (title text, font, state bits).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a target fact (retail's function lexicon lists it; see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks). The body is Zero Hour's
/// with BFME 2's list data layout and display string calls.
/// \see W3DGadgetListBoxImageDraw, drawListBoxText


/// \fn void W3DGadgetListBoxImageDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for list boxes with an image background: the
///        background image, optional title, then the rows.
///
/// The retail function lexicon (the callback-name table, see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks) lists this callback by name,
/// and slot 3 of each image-look list box window vtable calls it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The width loses the scroll bar's width whenever the list has one,
///   hidden or not. The colour look subtracts only a visible one, and three
///   more pixels.
/// - The image is slot 0 of the disabled, highlighted or enabled array, and
///   the title colours come from the window's text colours for the same
///   state. If the image exists it is stretched over the reduced width and
///   the full height, moved by the instance data's image offset.
/// - A title is drawn as in the colour look, one pixel in from the top left.
///   The area then starts one font height lower, and its height shrinks by
///   the font height plus one.
/// - The rows are drawn by drawListBoxText() starting four pixels below the
///   area top.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The art is slot 0 of the three draw-data arrays; IMAGEOFFSET moves the
/// background only, not the rows or the title. The selected-row images in
/// slots 1 to 4 apply here too.
///
/// \param window   The list box window.
/// \param instData Its instance data (title text, font, state bits, image
///                 offset).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a target fact (retail's function lexicon lists it; see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks). The body is Zero Hour's
/// with BFME 2's list data layout and display string calls.
/// \see W3DGadgetListBoxDraw, drawListBoxText


/// \file Gadget/W3DPushButton.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks
/// \brief W3D device layer: the push button draw routines and their text,
///        count and radial overlay helpers.
///
/// The image push button dispatcher is in a separate file and is described
/// in the gadget lists group. The file also defines a helper that draws a
/// numeric count on a button; this page does not describe it.


/// \fn void getButtonTextColors(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData, Color *textColor, Color *dropColor)
/// \brief Picks a push button's text colour and its text shadow colour from
///        the window's state.
///
/// Called by drawButtonText() and by the helper that draws a button's count.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A window that is not enabled gives the disabled text colour and text
/// border colour; otherwise a highlighted instance gives the highlighted
/// pair; otherwise the enabled pair. The selected bit is not consulted, so a
/// pushed-in button keeps its normal text colours. The text border colour is
/// used as the shadow colour.
///
/// \param window    The button window, which holds the three text colour
///                  pairs.
/// \param instData  Its instance data (the state bits).
/// \param textColor [out] Receives the text colour.
/// \param dropColor [out] Receives the shadow colour.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The pairs are the window's text colours from the layout's TEXTCOLOR
/// field, which holds the enabled, disabled and highlighted colour with a
/// border colour for each (see parseTextColor()).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is BFME 1's. The selection rule is read from the BFME 2 body and
/// is the one Zero Hour's drawButtonText uses inline.
/// \see drawButtonText


/// \fn static void drawButtonText(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draws a push button's label, centred on the button, wrapped to
///        the button's width.
///
/// File-static helper of W3DGadgetPushButtonDraw(),
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne() and W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawThree(),
/// which call it only when the instance data has text. The label is the
/// window's display string.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once when there is no display string or it is empty.
/// - Sets the string to wrap at the button's width, and to centre each
///   wrapped line when the window has the wrap-centred status.
/// - Takes the colours from getButtonTextColors(), gives the string the
///   window's font if it has another, and measures it.
/// - The position centres the measured text on the button, from the button's
///   screen position and size. It sets the text colour and draws with a
///   one-pixel shadow offset. The body sets no clip region of its own.
///
/// \param window   The button window.
/// \param instData Its instance data, which holds the display string and
///                 the status bits.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version has a special case for the status bit it calls
///       the shortcut-button bit: such a button's text is placed at the top
///       edge, two pixels in from the left. The BFME 2 body has no such
///       branch. The status bit with the same numeric value is the
///       radial-look switch in W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne() (see there);
///       that it is the same flag as Zero Hour's is not established.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The word wrap is re-set from the button's size on every draw, and the
/// string's font is switched to the window's font when it differs.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is Zero Hour's. The wrap, colour, font and centring steps are
/// read from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see getButtonTextColors


/// \fn static void drawRadialOverlay(const Image *image, ICoord2D *start, ICoord2D *size, Color color)
/// \brief Draws an overlay image centred on a radial button, scaled so that
///        a 48-pixel image covers the button.
///
/// File-static helper of W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne(), which calls it
/// for the pushed, highlighted and border overlays of radial buttons.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The button's half width and half height give a centre. Each half-extent
/// is then multiplied by the image's size over 48, so the drawn rectangle
/// is the button's size times image size over 48, centred on the button. An
/// image 48 pixels wide covers exactly the button's width, a larger one
/// overhangs it. The display draws the image in its alpha-blended mode,
/// tinted with the colour.
///
/// \param image The overlay image. Must not be null: its size is read.
/// \param start Top-left of the button in screen pixels.
/// \param size  Size of the button in pixels.
/// \param color Tint colour with alpha, as the window manager packs it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Overlay art for radial buttons is authored on a 48-pixel base, since 48
/// is a constant in the routine. The images are the mapped images named in
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne().
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a reconstruction label for a file-local function with no
/// retail symbol; the behaviour is read from the BFME 2 body. It is not a
/// donor name: Zero Hour has no radial overlay and no function of this name,
/// and BFME 1 has only a helper with the same centred, 48-based scaling under
/// an address-suffixed name.
/// \see W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne


/// \fn void W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawThree(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draws a horizontal three-piece image push button: a left end, a
///        repeating middle and a right end, then the label and extras.
///
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw() calls this for a button that has a middle
/// enabled image, after the dispatcher's own vertical-look test, unless the
/// dispatcher's overlay-state test sends it to
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne() instead. That test reads the
/// instance state word with the bit value of the overlay-state status flag;
/// it is not the window-status USE_OVERLAY_STATES flag that
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne() reads (see W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw()
/// in \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists). It is Zero Hour's routine with BFME 2's
/// display calls.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The three images come from the disabled, highlighted or enabled array
///   by the same state rule as other buttons, with the selected bit
///   choosing the selected slots (see the group description for the slots).
///   If any of the three is missing, nothing at all is drawn, not even the
///   label.
/// - The ends keep their images' widths. Between them the middle image is
///   tiled as many whole times as fit; the leftover gap is filled by one
///   more middle tile clipped to the gap, after which clipping is turned off.
///   The ends are drawn last, over the tiles. The image offset moves the
///   pieces. When the two ends together are not narrower than the button,
///   the button is simply split in half between the two end images.
/// - The label follows (drawButtonText), then the video buffer if the
///   instance data has one, drawn over the whole button.
/// - If the button has push button data: its overlay image, drawn over the
///   whole button in alpha mode; the clock, if requested, as a normal or
///   inverse clock-wipe in the stored colour and percentage, after which
///   the request is cleared and the data written back, so a clock lasts
///   one draw; and the extra outline, when enabled, drawn one pixel outside
///   the button.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The art is set per state in the draw data. The clock, overlay image and
/// outline are set by code (GadgetButtonDrawClock(), GadgetButtonSetBorder()
/// and the like), not by layout data. A draw consumes the clock request, so
/// code that wants a clock on screen sets it again for every frame; this
/// routine never clears the overlay image or the outline.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The routine relies on the display's clip region and on display helpers
/// for the clocks and image modes; this page does not describe those
/// helpers.
///
/// \param window   The push button window.
/// \param instData Its instance data (state bits, image offset, text, video
///                 buffer).
///
/// \note This routine does not draw the count label that the colour look and
///       the single-image look can draw. Zero Hour has no count label.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is not in retail's function lexicon; it follows the BFME 1 and Zero
/// Hour function of the same name that the dispatcher calls. The behaviour is
/// read from the BFME 2 body and agrees with Zero Hour's.
/// \see W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw, W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne,
///      drawButtonText


/// \fn void W3DGadgetPushButtonDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for colour-look push buttons: an outlined, filled
///        rectangle, the label, then the extras.
///
/// The retail function lexicon (the callback-name table, see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks) lists this callback by name,
/// and slot 3 of the colour push button's window vtable calls it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The colour and border colour are slot 0 (normal) or slot 1 (selected,
///   by the instance state's selected bit) of the disabled, highlighted or
///   enabled array.
/// - The border colour draws an outline of the whole button and the colour
///   a fill one pixel inside it. Either may be undefined.
/// - The label follows (drawButtonText), then the video buffer if the
///   instance data has one, then the push button data extras: the overlay
///   image over the whole button, the clock request (drawn and then
///   cleared), a count label when the data enables it, and the extra
///   outline one pixel outside the button.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Colours come from slots 0 and 1 of the three draw-data arrays.
///   Zero Hour: a CHECK_LIKE button (see GadgetButtonEnableCheckLike())
///   flips its selected state bit on each click (GadgetPushButton.cpp) and
///   holds its on/off state in that bit (GadgetCheckLikeButtonIsChecked()).
///   This routine picks the selected slot from that bit, so such a button
///   shows the selected colours while it is on. BFME 2's check-like setter
///   was not read for this page.
/// - The clock, overlay, count and outline are set by code and are not
///   layout data.
///
/// \param window   The push button window.
/// \param instData Its instance data.
///
/// \note Compared with Zero Hour's routine of the same name, the differences
///       found include that BFME 2 draws the overlay image through a display
///       call with an explicit alpha mode and adds the count label between
///       the clock and the outline.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a target fact (retail's function lexicon lists it; see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks). The behaviour is read
/// from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne, drawButtonText,
///      GadgetButtonSetBorder, GadgetButtonDrawClock


/// \fn void W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draws a push button from a single image per state, with the
///        overlay-state tints, the radial look, clocks, flashing, the label
///        and the extras.
///
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw() calls this for a button that the
/// dispatcher's own vertical-look test does not take and that has no middle
/// enabled image, and for one that has a middle enabled image when the
/// dispatcher's overlay-state test passes. That test reads the instance state
/// word with the bit value of the overlay-state status flag, so it should not
/// be equated with the window-status USE_OVERLAY_STATES flag that this routine
/// itself reads; which state bit has that value, and whether anything sets
/// it, was not traced here (see W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw() in
/// \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists). It is the most elaborate button routine.
/// Zero Hour's version was smaller: the additions found in BFME 2 include the
/// radial look, the count label, more overlay art and two more status bits.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Image. The default is slot 0 of the enabled array. When the button uses
///   neither the overlay-state status nor the status the reconstruction calls
///   no-state-images, the state picks a slot as in the colour look: disabled
///   gives slot 0 or 1 of the disabled array, highlighted slot 0 or 1 of
///   the highlighted array, and an enabled, not highlighted, selected button
///   takes slot 1 of the highlighted array (Zero Hour does the same). The
///   image is stretched over the button, moved by the instance data's image
///   offset.
/// - Overlay states. For a button with the overlay-state status (and not
///   no-state-images), a disabled one that is not marked not-ready is drawn
///   in grayscale mode, or, with the always-colour status, in colour
///   multiplied by a dark grey. In BFME 2 a flag in the button data darkens
///   the grayscale case further. This is how one piece of colour art serves
///   every state.
/// - Radial look. The status bit with the same value as Zero Hour's
///   shortcut-button bit is the radial-look switch in the BFME 2 body;
///   whether it is the same flag as Zero Hour's is not established. The draw
///   mode becomes radial grayscale (if it was grayscale) or solid. The image
///   draw is bracketed by render-state helper calls, and a shape is drawn
///   through the display before it; that this is a stencil-style mask is an
///   inference from the call pattern. What the helpers do is not described
///   here.
/// - Extras, in order: the label (drawButtonText), the video buffer, the
///   overlay image, the clock request, the extra outline (not for radial
///   buttons) and the count label if enabled. The clock request is cleared
///   whenever it was set, even if nothing was drawn for it. A normal clock is
///   drawn when its percentage is above zero. An inverse clock is drawn when
///   its percentage is below 100, as two radial clock overlay images scaled
///   to 46 over 48 of the button, the first in the stored clock colour and
///   the second in a colour taken from global data. That colour differs for
///   a local player with one particular player flag set; the flag's meaning
///   is not established here, and with no local player the colour is zero.
/// - Flashing. A flashing button that is not radial gets the highlighted
///   cameo overlay drawn over the extras. Zero Hour draws its pushed-state
///   cameo overlay for flashing instead.
/// - State overlays. For buttons with the overlay-state status that are
///   enabled: highlighted and selected draws the pushed overlay,
///   highlighted alone the highlighted overlay, and both end the routine, so
///   a highlighted radial button gets no border overlay. A button that is
///   selected but not highlighted gets the pushed overlay unless it is
///   radial. Any radial button that has not ended earlier finishes with its
///   border overlay. Radial buttons use drawRadialOverlay() with the radial
///   variants of the overlay images; other buttons stretch the cameo
///   overlays over the button. These overlays are tinted white, or dark grey
///   if the gray-overlay status is set.
/// - Art names. On the first call the routine looks up seven mapped images
///   by fixed name, once: the cameo push and cameo hilited icons, the radial
///   push, over and border images, and the two radial clock overlays. The
///   pointers are kept for the life of the process and are used unchecked.
///   Zero Hour looked up only the two cameo icons and skipped the state
///   overlays unless both were found.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the per-state images (the layout's draw data) and the
///   image offset, as for other buttons. With either the overlay-state or
///   the no-state-images status only slot 0 of the enabled array is used.
/// - Status flags. The routine tests the overlay-state, always-colour,
///   not-ready, flashing, radial, no-state-images and gray-overlay statuses.
///   Of these, USE_OVERLAY_STATES and ALWAYS_COLOR are tokens in the
///   reconstruction's token table, which is Zero Hour's list and so
///   unconfirmed for BFME 2 (see the group description). FLASHING is
///   consulted here; who sets it in BFME 2 was not traced (in Zero Hour the
///   control bar's code sets and clears it at run time). NOT_READY and
///   ALWAYS_COLOR are set and cleared by the BFME 2 control bar code named
///   in the group description. For control bar command buttons, a layout
///   value is not the whole story: that description names the BFME 2
///   control bar code that sets the overlay-state bit, and the code that,
///   for the command windows it processes (it skips a hidden window whose
///   command is hidden), clears the not-ready, always-colour,
///   no-state-images and gray-overlay bits on each pass and sets some of
///   them again, so on those windows the four are run-time state. No
///   layout tokens for the radial, no-state-images and gray-overlay bits
///   were confirmed.
/// - The cameo and radial overlay icons are looked up by name, so a mod
///   supplies them by defining mapped images named Cameo_push,
///   Cameo_hilited, RadialPush, RadialOver, RadialBorder,
///   RadialClockOverlay1 and RadialClockOverlay2. The control bar also
///   looks up the five Radial names when it preloads button art, which
///   suggests that radial buttons are command bar buttons; that is an
///   inference and is not confirmed here.
/// - Clock, overlay image, count and outline are set by code, not by layout
///   data. The tints, the dark grey values and the 46 over 48 scale are
///   fixed.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - A renderer has to support the image draw modes (solid, grayscale,
///   alpha and a radial grayscale), the clock draws and whatever the radial
///   look's helpers set up; these are display functions that this page does
///   not describe.
/// - The overlay icon pointers are cached on the first call; refresh them if
///   the mapped images are reloaded.
/// - Nothing scales the image for resolution; it fills the button.
///
/// \param window   The push button window.
/// \param instData Its instance data.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's window status enumeration ends at the shortcut-button bit. The
///       reconstruction's no-state-images and gray-overlay bits (the two
///       highest bits) have no Zero Hour counterpart, and no layout token
///       for them was confirmed here. BFME 2's control bar update clears
///       and sets both on its command buttons (see the group description).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is not in retail's function lexicon; it follows the BFME 1 and Zero
/// Hour function of the same name that the dispatcher calls. The behaviour is
/// read from the BFME 2 body and compared with Zero Hour's. The status bit
/// names and the description of the radial look are the reconstruction's
/// labels for what the body does.
/// \see W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw, W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawThree,
///      drawRadialOverlay, drawButtonText


/// \file Gadget/W3DVerticalSlider.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks
/// \brief W3D device layer: the image-look vertical slider draw callback.
///
/// Retail contains one body for the colour-look draw of both slider
/// orientations (the two source functions compile to identical code), so the
/// horizontal slider's colour draw function serves vertical sliders too. For
/// the status of individual functions, use the project's progress tools. The
/// draw function calls slider draw-data accessors that the slider gadget
/// header defines inline; the compiler emits copies of those header-defined
/// getters in this unit, and they are not described here.


/// \fn void W3DGadgetVerticalSliderImageDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for image-look vertical sliders: the track, built
///        from a top end, a bottom end and repeating centre pieces.
///
/// The retail function lexicon (the callback-name table, see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks) lists this callback by name,
/// and slot 3 of the image-look vertical slider's window vtable calls it. It
/// draws the track only. Zero Hour: the thumb is a separate child window, a
/// push button created by the window manager when it builds a slider, so it
/// is drawn by the push button draw functions. That the BFME 2 slider builds
/// its thumb the same way was not checked.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The four images (top end, bottom end, centre, small centre) are slots 0
///   to 3 of the disabled, highlighted or enabled array. If any is missing
///   nothing is drawn.
/// - If the two end images together are at least as tall as the window, the
///   track is too short for a middle: each end is stretched to its side of
///   a split line at half the window height below the window top, each at
///   its own image width. The split line does not move with the vertical
///   image offset. The offset moves the top edge of the top image and the
///   bottom edge of the bottom image: the top image runs from the window
///   top plus the offset down to the split line, and the bottom image runs
///   from the split line down to the window bottom plus the offset. With a
///   non-zero offset the two ends are therefore not equal heights, and with
///   no offset each is half the window height.
/// - Otherwise the centre image is tiled downward at its own size between
///   the ends, as many whole tiles as fit. Small centre tiles then fill the
///   remaining gap, one more than fits so that the last one runs underneath
///   the bottom end; they are drawn at the centre image's width and their
///   own height. The ends are drawn last, over the tiles, at their own
///   sizes. In this tiled case the instance data's image offset shifts every
///   piece.
/// - Nothing clips the tiles. The extra small tile is drawn under the bottom
///   end and is hidden only to the extent that the bottom end covers it: it
///   can reach down past the top edge of the bottom end by up to its own
///   height, so the bottom end hides it only if that image is at least that
///   tall and covers the tile's width.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The four images come from slots 0 to 3 of the three draw-data arrays. A
/// slider needs all four in the state being drawn or its track vanishes.
/// The track's height is the window's height from the layout. When the ends
/// fit, the pieces keep their native sizes and the centre pieces are tiled
/// to fill that height; when they do not, each end is stretched to its side of
/// the half-height split line. The vertical image offset moves the top edge
/// of the top image and the bottom edge of the bottom image but not the
/// split line, so the ends are each half the window height only with no
/// vertical image offset.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Sizes come from the art, so the track tiles at native pixel size and is
/// not resolution independent.
///
/// \param window   The slider window.
/// \param instData Its instance data (state bits, image offset).
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is a target fact (retail's function lexicon lists it; see
/// \ref grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks). The behaviour is read
/// from the BFME 2 body, which matches Zero Hour's W3DVerticalSlider.cpp.
/// \see W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderImageDraw


/// \file GUI/W3DGameWindow_initBorders.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_w3d_gadget_draw_callbacks
/// \brief W3D device layer: the window border art lookup and the border
///        blit that consumes it.
///
/// The file also holds W3DGameWindow::blitBorderRect, which draws a border
/// from the pieces that initBorders() finds.


/// \fn void initBorders(void)
/// \brief Looks up the twelve border pieces by name and marks the border art
///        as loaded.
///
/// The border blit (W3DGameWindow::blitBorderRect) calls this the first time
/// it runs and never again, so the lookup happens on the first border blit.
/// A window with the border status (and not the see-through status) gets its
/// border from GameWindowManager::drawWindow, which calls
/// GameWindow::winDrawBorder. That hands the window to the window drawer
/// object's border routine, which chooses a rectangle by window style and
/// calls the blit. The styles that reach the blit are push buttons, radio
/// buttons, entry fields, scroll list boxes, static text, progress bars,
/// user windows and tab controls. Styles with no blit case (check boxes,
/// sliders and others, such as tab panes and combo boxes) draw no border
/// here. The style names are Zero Hour's numbering, which the case values in
/// the BFME 2 border routine fit; retail's own style-name table was not read
/// for this page. The tab control draw callbacks call GameWindow::winDrawBorder
/// themselves as well.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each of four corners (upper left, upper right, lower left, lower
/// right) and for the left, right, top and bottom edges, each as a full piece
/// and a short half-length piece, the function asks the mapped image
/// collection (TheMappedImageCollection) for an image by a fixed name and
/// stores the result in a file-level table. The names are BorderCornerUL,
/// BorderCornerUR, BorderCornerLL, BorderCornerLR, BorderLeft,
/// BorderLeftShort, BorderRight, BorderRightShort, BorderTop,
/// BorderTopShort, BorderBottom and BorderBottomShort. Zero Hour's lookup
/// returns null for an unknown name, which leaves that entry empty. Finally
/// the loaded flag is set, even if every lookup failed, so a failed lookup
/// is never retried.
///
/// The blit tiles the edge pieces 20 pixels at a time, finishes each edge
/// with half-length pieces, and places 20-pixel corner images with fixed
/// offsets around the window. These sizes live in the blit, not in the
/// art.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Window borders are art, not code: defining mapped images with these
///   twelve names restyles every window whose style draws its border
///   through the blit (see above; not check boxes or sliders).
/// - Defining them after the first border blit has no effect, because the
///   table is filled once.
/// - Edge pieces are drawn at 20 pixels, so border art is authored at that
///   size.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The lookup is cached for the life of the process; a port that reloads
/// the image collection must reset the loaded flag or refresh the table.
/// All border geometry is in fixed pixels and does not scale with
/// resolution.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. Zero Hour declares the function static; the
///       reconstructed definition is not declared static, and retail linkage
///       cannot be established, so none is claimed here.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The name is Zero Hour's, reached through the BFME 1 reconstruction. The
/// twelve image names are string literals in the BFME 2 body. The only
/// caller found is the border blit.
/// \see W3DGadgetTabControlDraw, W3DGadgetTabControlImageDraw
